  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1131 PM CST THU NOV 12 2026\n\nThe second cold front is still expected to push through the\nregion overnight and into tomorrow morning, with the secondary\nsurge of colder temperatures following right behind it. Afternoon\nhighs tomorrow will only range in the low to upper-50s, with some\nof the coldest temperatures of the season expected Friday night.\nHere, skies are expected to clear behind the front, with winds\ncompletely decoupling overnight. This will allow for widespread\nradiational cooling, with lows falling into the upper-20s in some\nof our northern zones. A Freeze Watch remains in effect for this\ntimeframe, with subsequent upgrades into a Freeze Warning coming\nin future forecast packages.\n\nOvernight lows are expected to remain very cold through the\nweekend, with afternoon highs continuing to range in the low to\nupper-50s. By Sunday, a closed low will develop off to our west,\nmoving into the region during the afternoon hours on Monday. This\nlow may introduce some low-end rain chances in our southern\nzones, but confidence here is currently low based on low timing\nand intensity. Otherwise, the region will remain dry for the next\nseven days.\n\nAs we enter into the middle of the new workweek, the upper-level\npattern will begin to break down, with ridging building back in\noverhead. This ridge will allow for temperatures to begin to\nclimb, with afternoon highs getting back into the upper-60s and\nlow-70s. Overnight lows, on the other hand, will remain on the\ncool side, as cold temperatures remain filtering down the\nAppalachian Mountains under a CAD wedge.\n\n/44/",
